      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the positive value of \\( t \\) for which \\( |{-4+ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we start by recalling the formula for the magnitude (or modulus) of a complex number. For a complex number \\( a + bi \\), the magnitude is given by:\n\n\\[\n|a + bi| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\). Therefore, we can identify \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\). The magnitude of \\( -4 + ti \\) is:\n\n\\[\n|-4 + ti| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\). So, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nNext, we square both sides of the equation to eliminate the square root:\n\n\\[\n(-4)^2 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the squares, we get:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\( t^2 \\), we subtract 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we obtain:\n\n\\[\nt = \\pm 6\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\), we have:\n\n\\[\nt = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
